### The naive calculation of the fiscal effort

To stabilise public debt at 120% of GDP, with an implicit rate of 2.3% (the nominal implicit rate on French public debt in 2026) and a current nominal growth rate of 2.2% per year (growth in 2026 as forecast by IFE\|OFCE in October 2026, see @tbl-optimal1), a primary balance of 0.1 point of GDP is required. The gap with the primary deficit expected for 2026 is therefore 3.0 points of GDP (€92 billion at 2026 GDP) (row 5 of @tbl-naif).

If, instead of 2026 growth, one uses long term potential growth (1.1% per year in volume, with 1.8% inflation); instead of the primary deficit, the structural primary deficit (i.e. adjusted for the cycle, −2.2% of GDP in 2026); and, for the interest rate, the current weighted rate, the debt-stabilising balance is −0.7 point of GDP and the gap with the structural primary balance is 1.5 points of GDP (€46 billion at 2026 GDP, row 8 of @tbl-naif). Using the same assumptions but with the long-term rate (an interest–growth differential of 0.5%), the fiscal effort amounts to €85 billion (row 12 of @tbl-naif).

@tbl-naif covers 12 configurations, depending on whether one starts from the primary or the structural (i.e. cyclically adjusted) deficit; on whether one uses the rate observed on markets, the weighted average rate or an estimated long-term rate (based on an interest–growth differential $r-g$ assumed to be 0.5%); and on the nominal growth benchmark (expected volume growth and GDP deflator growth in 2026, or potential growth and the central bank's inflation target). Each of these combinations is nonetheless unsatisfactory, all the more so as the estimated fiscal efforts range from €46 billion to €192 billion, a very wide interval.


::: {#tbl-naif .cell tbl-cap='Fiscal effort 2026–2032, naive approach'}
::: {.cell-output-display}

