## The IFE\|OFCE autumn 2026 forecast and the 2027 Budget Bill {#sec-main}

The "optimal" adjustment — which seeks to minimise the (negative) impact on activity subject to meeting the objective of debt at 120% of GDP in 2032 [@sec-annexe1] — leads to an impulse of −1.3 points of GDP in 2028 (@tbl-optimal1) and a cumulative impulse of 3.4 points of GDP by 2032 (€105 billion at 2026 GDP). This result stems from a large initial deficit (5.1% in 2025, 5.5% in 2026, 5.3% in 2027) and from a short-dated debt objective (2032). The adjustment (the primary impulse) in 2026 is −0.5%, which corresponds to the IFE\|OFCE autumn 2026 forecast [@prev2609] (prev2609). This forecast was published on 15 October 2026, before the difficult negotiations surrounding the vote on the 2027 budget.

The prev2609 forecast projects a deficit of 5.5% of GDP in 2026, in line with the government's communication and with the assessment of the High Council of Public Finance (*Haut Conseil des finances publiques*), and higher than the assumption of @ratav (5.0% in July 2026). The IFE\|OFCE forecast differsas regards growth (0.4% in 2026), owing, among other things, to a large multiplier effect. Despite an impulse of −0.5 point of GDP, the government deficit widens by 0.4 point of GDP between 2025 and 2026. This is due to a sharp rise in interest payments (+0.3 point of GDP)est payments (+0.3 point of GDP) and to particularly weak economic conditions resulting from higher energy prices and political uncertainty.

For 2027, IFE\|OFCE anticipates a combination of factors similar to that of 2026: activity is weighed down by a sluggish international environment, by caution among domestic economic agents at a time of political tension, and by a fairly strong negative impulse. The rise in interest payments continues (+0.3 point in 2027). With a budget approved by a political majority all but ruled out, the budget results from a complex compromise, combining a special stopgap law (*loi spéciale*) and the absence of a successful censure motion, as in 2026.

Beyond 2027, the simulation proceeds by determining an "optimal" economic policy that achieves an objective (*e.g.* a debt target at a given date) while minimising the impact on activity over all the periods concerned (@sec-annexe5). The adjustment anticipated for 2027 by IFE\|OFCE in October 2026 is loosely close to an "optimal" adjustment (cumulative over 2026 and 2027 of 1.3% of GDP opposed to 1.9% of GDP in the "true" optimal case (@tbl-maintenant)) and is consistent with the rules of the Stability and Growth Pact (SGP). The adjustment then gradually eases. This is partly due to the easing of sovereign rates in 2029, which allows interest payments to stabilise relatively (at around 3.4 points of GDP in 2032, or 1.5 points of GDP once the inflation tax is taken into account).

This adjustment results in a widening of the output gap between 2026 and 2028. From 2029 onwards, impulses are smaller and, owing to the multiplier dynamics, the adjustment has less effect on the output gap, which narrows slowly before turning positive from 2033 (@fig-optimal, blue line).

The peak in public debt in this scenario is 123.1% of GDP (@tbl-optimal1), although the confidence interval is fairly wide (@fig-optimal), particularly for public debt. Note that this interval is constructed under specific assumptions [see @timbeau2025] and should be interpreted accordingly. To simplify to the extreme, it reflects the historical variability of growth, while assuming that mean growth is stable and that there is little autocorrelation in this process (i.e. that it is stationary). This assumption implies mean reversion of the output gap and therefore limits the amplification of a median deviation of the debt path.

In this scenario, public debt peaks in 2028 and the French government deficit falls below 3.0% from 2031. In the long run, the deficit is above 3% because, at this level of debt, the debt-stabilising balance is around −3.3% (−2.75% ⨉ 120%). This leads to a hump-shaped profile for the government balance: a higher balance must first be reached to reduce debt, before returning to the debt-stabilising deficit[^7].

[^7]: This property is common in optimal control problems when the available instrument acts on acceleration and the objective is defined in terms of both position (the debt target) and velocity (debt no longer changes).


