## Detailed simulations {#sec-resvar}

### Optimal adjustment in 2026 and 2027 {#sec-maintenant}

@tbl-maintenant describes a world slightly different from the IFE\|OFCE forecast [@prev2609]. Instead of the forecast impulses, an optimal impulse is assumed. Applying optimal fiscal policy from 2026 yields a standard result: *backloading* (deferring adjustment over time) is justified only by a strong preference for the present, by a preference for impulses that vary as little as possible, or by fiscal multipliers that vary over the cycle. As none of these features is present in the simulation, the optimal impulse is that of a sharp recession in 2026, followed by a more gradual adjustment thereafter. The trough in activity occurs in 2027 and is less pronounced than that of 2028 in the prev2609 scenario (@tbl-optimal1). The average output loss between 2026 and 2032 is 1.4 points of GDP, compared with 1.5 points in the prev2609 scenario.

The fiscal effort is appreciably lower in this scenario (3.2% of GDP cumulated, compared with 3.4%). An immediate negative impulse curbs the rise in debt and in cumulative interest payments. This strategy is broadly equivalent (in terms of fiscal effort, output cost and unemployment) to simply stabilising debt in 2032 (@sec-annexe3) — although debt ends up higher in the latter scenario.


