@fig-taux shows the sensitivity of the effort to the interest–growth differential. The sensitivity amounts to €20 billion (at 2026 GDP) per point of differential for public debt of around 120% of GDP (115.6% at the start of the period, 120% at the end). If debt were hypothetically lower (around 60% of GDP; see @sec-d60 for details of the assumptions), the cost would be halved, at €9.5 billion (at 2026 GDP). Because the current (or implicit) differential is negative at the start of the simulation period, higher public debt reduces the snowball effect. Further into the simulation, this effect fades and the differential rises under the weight of the current interest rate. For a differential of 0.5% and a 2032 horizon, the two effects offset exactly each other.

Were one to start from a higher implicit differential, or to run the same simulations over a longer horizon, the advantage of a low implicit differential would disappear.


