## What if debt had been at 60%? {#sec-d60}



Here we rerun all the simulations of @tbl-sum under a strong assumption: public debt in 2025 is 55.6% of GDP instead of 115.6%, i.e. a downward shift of 60 points of GDP.

Instead of a target of 120% of GDP, we target debt of 60% of GDP. The simulation parameters are unchanged, and the conventions of @tbl-sum are retained. In particular, the government deficit in 2025 is the same (5.1% of GDP) and interest payments in 2026 are reduced in line with the new debt-to-GDP ratio — and therefore roughly halved. The sovereign rate is also lower, since the interest–growth differential ($r-g$) is assumed to be zero — a rather favourable assumption. Between 2012 and 2022, this differential was negative (see @tip-dyndet) when measured using the market sovereign rate — and it remains negative today when measured using the implicit rate. The entire interest–growth differential is thus attributed to the gap between debt and the Stability and Growth Pact reference value, which is an overstatement but allows this effect to be isolated.

This thought experiment is admittedly artificial, but it is useful for gauging the constraint that the level of debt places on restoring public finances to balance (in terms of the primary deficit) and on a moderately ambitious medium-term debt objective.

@tbl-sumd60 summarises the results of these simulations. The main finding is that halving public debt reduce slightly the cost of fiscal adjustment. With a zero interest–growth differential, the fiscal cost is 0.1 point of GDP lower than that computed for the observed level of debt (@tbl-sum); for a differential of +0.8 percentage point, the gap is +0.0% of GDP.


